-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161210}] :question/id #uuid "14118443-b386-51d2-bcd8-a6f6bd250385" :question/math-academy-id "q-95064" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x) = 9x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 6x_{2}^{2}$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178f1a53-b088-503c-9e1c-e483af83c450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357956} {:answer/id #uuid "397463a5-5679-57da-9a4f-269e65a0067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358258} {:answer/id #uuid "63801535-5db3-5fa4-adf2-a0a612b71714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358617} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9887c3-7818-533a-a7be-08484715473d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359004} {:answer/id #uuid "e74b23e9-0a62-589c-8a56-1890f0262afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63801535-5db3-5fa4-adf2-a0a612b71714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358617} :answer-field/id #uuid "71057f21-bf84-51ad-b440-b6a3ef2a0bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "574f8096-6cd3-5243-b06d-c1422dfabc1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95071" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30d23a1d-c5cb-5263-97ae-c1d2aee936d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358186} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc887c-1f90-584f-b269-2479e33dc2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358522} {:answer/id #uuid "831b1872-6d21-50bd-badf-8c555a95dd41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358904} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d95938-4e3e-5324-958a-18c961de80a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359165} {:answer/id #uuid "f720c207-dde0-55ae-835a-5c47400c2515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -15\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58cc887c-1f90-584f-b269-2479e33dc2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f052b1-64d0-5f2d-bf08-da51612219c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85c2527c-a204-5bec-a809-539cc736fb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-95092" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}14-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-20\\lambda +75 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =15,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-15\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc8877a5-3ad2-52bb-a064-0496ec64af0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161217} {:answer/id #uuid "de86953f-2dc6-5b88-b2b6-6650bf357093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161218} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb9df63-3e91-5305-ab66-7239c594b50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161219} {:answer/id #uuid "463dc66e-e8fb-5326-b371-3f4a5d8ec9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161220} {:answer/id #uuid "698774d3-bbc4-5952-8a59-a504e856805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161221}] :answer-field/id #uuid "72d9a893-e9aa-5f30-88df-e785d8b3a106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "927834b7-3d18-5db5-abab-f666bc5af8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394998} {:answer/id #uuid "26af6070-1f6f-51b7-9850-35fb204fd9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394999} {:answer/id #uuid "64bcd310-100e-5d4a-9083-894659694caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395000} {:answer/id #uuid "79ebf064-b4d2-5fc0-acb8-3c83ecd66e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395001} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8de7cc-e082-5310-94db-9f5a79a7e15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "927834b7-3d18-5db5-abab-f666bc5af8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394998} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd2f1c2-56b9-525a-98ce-2d7df8ee2c78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "00c817da-6580-5c3d-a2f1-9c059dd42da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-95095" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ad1ec4-5285-5fc0-b181-ec74835f1cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357919} {:answer/id #uuid "c04b5f9c-0cfa-53f9-8092-b81696f43652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359436} {:answer/id #uuid "d34be52c-cc27-5490-b827-286fa8c7ab5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359598} {:answer/id #uuid "d90208da-a536-53d7-b928-c9043cda2503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359652} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8f298b-e39d-59ad-bbd3-20b6206af75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04b5f9c-0cfa-53f9-8092-b81696f43652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359436} :answer-field/id #uuid "d638fe3b-55f5-5a05-8898-0a3158620a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06d1c40d-cb10-5fdd-9559-6a4e07bdb150" :question/math-academy-id "q-95101" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07cbbfc-0f61-55e8-afe9-b675dc74f24e" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 3D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0251ccc3-dc7a-5e13-aae7-b6bf4c2f5b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357779} {:answer/id #uuid "0a779e87-5b4d-55ed-a0f0-60ea11c6dfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357842} {:answer/id #uuid "25fd46a2-f522-5116-b50b-fc0046027abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358095} {:answer/id #uuid "63ebedd3-8e4f-51bc-9e7f-e490669a2a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358625} {:answer/id #uuid "f5660179-7c61-57c9-8384-708cc6542132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63ebedd3-8e4f-51bc-9e7f-e490669a2a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358625} :answer-field/id #uuid "697f9656-075e-5af9-8ad6-64fd9e96840f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0cd3fae-972c-5f2d-a33e-05af0737f6a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95103" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" :question/worked-solution "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}]}
